A nested anonymous aggregate gets the enclosing offset.

THIS IS THE P151 FAULT, AND IT WAS NEVER IN tccgen.

For five rounds this series recorded the fault as "next() returns and its caller
in tccgen faults", and every attempt to explain it looked for a codegen bug on
the token path. It was a member offset, three functions away, and it had been
corrupting every symbol tcc ever created.

    before   exit: 139   SIGNAL 11
    after    exit: 1     no signal

mc-tcc now preprocesses, parses and generates code for its input and stops at
the linker, complaining that crt1.o and crti.o are not present -- which they
are not, in the local harness. That is a different problem and a much later
one.

WHAT WAS WRONG. `members` is the SIBLING chain: build_member sets
i->members = last. So the flattening loop for an anonymous aggregate

    while (iterator != NULL) {
        iterator->offset = iterator->offset + offset;
        iterator = iterator->members;
    }

visits every member of ONE aggregate, and only one. A nested anonymous struct
appears on that chain as a single entry whose own members hang off
entry->type->members -- a different list -- and those never received the
enclosing offset. They kept offsets relative to the inner aggregate.

tcc's Sym is exactly that shape:

    typedef struct Sym {
        int v;                  /* offset 0  */
        unsigned short r;
        struct SymAttr a;
        union {
            struct { int c; int sym_scope; ... };    /* c resolved to 0 */
            int jnext;
        };
    } Sym;

`c` aliased `v`. And sym_push2 is:

    s->v = v;          /* token written        */
    s->type.t = t;
    s->c = c;          /* c is 0 for a field -- wipes the token */

so the third store erased what the first had just written. EVERY symbol tcc
created came back with v == 0. check_fields then computed

    table_ident[v - TOK_IDENT]   ->   table_ident[0 - 256]   ->   index -256

read NULL out of it, and dereferenced that. SIGSEGV.

The fix recurses into a nested anonymous aggregate. It also makes the offsets
agree with lookup_member, which ALREADY recursed into anonymous members -- so
the two halves of the same rule had disagreed since anonymous aggregates were
added.

HOW IT WAS FOUND, BECAUSE THE ROUTE IS THE LESSON.

Bisecting downward with hand-placed write() markers, one frame at a time:

    tccgen_compile      next() completes, decl(VT_CONST) faults
    decl                parse_btype entered, never returns
    parse_btype         depth 1 -> 2 -> 1 -> 2 -> 1, all returns clean
    struct_decl         all six members of __builtin_va_list parse correctly
    struct_decl tail    M13 attributes done, M14 not reached
    check_fields        v == 0, index -256, ts == NULL
    sym_push2           P2 s->v=333, P3 s->v=333, P4 s->v=0

The last line is the whole diagnosis. Four probes on four consecutive stores.

FOUR TIMES DURING THIS HUNT A CONCLUSION WAS DRAWN FROM A STALE marks.txt.
local-tcc.sh does not stop when micro-c fails to compile the unit, and the
runs were piped to /dev/null or through `head`, which closes the pipe and kills
the script before it ever runs the binary. The file was eleven minutes older
than the binary beside it and said what the previous build had said. Three
separate "findings" were retracted:

  - "none of the 9 call sites fire"     -- the build had failed on zdbg being
                                          used above its definition
  - "continue jumps to the function top" -- stale; a difftest case for it passes
  - the frame/depth probes              -- stale twice over

Every conclusion in this patch comes from a run where marks.txt was verified
NEWER than mc-tcc. That check costs one line and should have been there from
the first probe:

    ls -l --time-style=+%H:%M:%S mc-tcc marks.txt

MEASURED

    tcc           SIGSEGV -> exit 1, no signal. THE FAULT IS CLOSED.
    difftest      60 pass / 0 fail / 1 known gap, both architectures
    case 62       the construct in 30 lines, red before and green after
    vocabulary    clean on five architectures
    corpus        423 of 426 on aarch64

WHAT IS NEXT AND NOT DONE HERE: crt1.o and crti.o. mc-tcc reaches the linker
and cannot find the C runtime objects. Whether that is a gap in the local
harness or the next real problem has not been established, and is not claimed
either way.

